 lining the balcony railings — are **yellow**. This is consistent across all frames showing the stage (e.g., Frames 1–3, 4–13, 15–16, 17–20, 23–24).\n\n---\n\n**5) Color of large logos/emblems:**\n\nThe large “N” logo on the stage floor is **white** on a **blue** background (Frames 4–13, 17–20, 26–30). The emblem on the banner above the organ is **gold/yellow** on a **blue** field (Frames 4–13, 17–20, 26–30).\n\n---\n\n**6) Consistency of color patterns across the stage:**\n\nThe color pattern is **highly consistent** across all frames. The stage maintains a unified palette of **blue flooring**, **yellow floral backdrops**, and **white/gold logos**. Even in close-ups (e.g., Frames 1–3, 15–16, 23–24), the yellow floral wall and blue stage elements remain visible, confirming the cohesive design.\n\n---\n\n**Verification of Answer \'2\' ("Blue and red"):**\n\nThe claim that the primary colors are “blue and red” is **incorrect**.\n\n- **Blue** is accurate — it is the dominant color of the stage flooring and logo background.\n- **Red** is **not** a primary color in the *stage decoration design*. While red appears in the **audience seating** (Frames 21–22, 26–30) and in **some attendees’ attire** (e.g., Frame 14 — Queen Silvia’s red gown), it is **not part of the stage’s decorative scheme**. The stage itself — including flooring, backdrop, floral arrangements, and logos — does not feature red as a design element.\n\n---\n\n✅ **Final Answer:**\n\nThe main color scheme of the entire stage decoration design is **blue and yellow/gold**, **not** blue and red. Therefore, answer \'2\' ("Blue and red") is **incorrect**. The correct primary colors used throughout the stage design are **blue and yellow**.'}, {'batch_start': 60, 'batch_end': 66, 'image_paths': ['/mnt/ssd/data/lvbench/videos_processed/YlQugR7KSKg/frames/frame_3946.jpg', '/mnt/ssd/data/lvbench/videos_processed/YlQugR7KSKg/frames/frame_3947.jpg', '/mnt/ssd/data/lvbench/videos_processed/YlQugR7KSKg/frames/frame_3948.jpg